            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a hunt, then the Latin for a beast."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: palma, palmae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "palma, palmae — \"the palm of victory; the palm of the hand; a palm tree\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Aurīga palmam et corōnam accēpit. The charioteer received the palm and a crown."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine. The flat of the hand, then the palm tree, whose branch was given to winners."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "palma is inherited from a Proto-Indo-European form for \"the flat of the hand\" (EtymDB), matching Greek palamē. The palm tree was named from its hand-like leaves. English palm comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
